The Nautilus DevOps team is gearing up to deploy applications on a Kubernetes cluster for migration purposes. 
A team member has been tasked with creating a ReplicaSet outlined below:

Create a ReplicaSet using nginx image with latest tag (ensure to specify as nginx:latest) and name it nginx-replicaset.

Apply labels: app as nginx_app, type as front-end.

Name the container nginx-container. Ensure the replica count is 4.

Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

SOLUTION
Creating the ReplicaSet
Here's the manifest that meets all the requirements, followed by the command to apply it.

Step 1: Create the manifest file
bash
cat <<EOF > nginx-replicaset.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: nginx-replicaset
  labels:
    app: nginx_app
    type: front-end
spec:
  replicas: 4
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx_app
      type: front-end
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx_app
        type: front-end
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx-container
          image: nginx:latest
EOF
Key points on the manifest:

name: nginx-replicaset — matches the required ReplicaSet name.

replicas: 4 — sets the replica count to 4.

selector.matchLabels must match the pod template labels, otherwise the API server rejects it.

Labels app: nginx_app and type: front-end are applied to both the ReplicaSet and the pods.

Container is named nginx-container with image nginx:latest.

Step 2: Apply the manifest
bash
kubectl apply -f nginx-replicaset.yaml
Step 3: Verify
bash
# Confirm the ReplicaSet exists with the right replica count
kubectl get replicaset nginx-replicaset

# Confirm 4 pods are running with the correct labels and image
kubectl get pods -l app=nginx_app,type=front-end -o wide

# Detailed check (labels, container name, image)
kubectl describe replicaset nginx-replicaset
Expected output from kubectl get replicaset nginx-replicaset:

text
NAME               DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE
nginx-replicaset   4         4         4       ...
And from the pods query you should see 4 pods with names like nginx-replicaset-xxxxx, all in Running status.

